{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize small-scale drug possession is ending as a new statute reclassifies such acts as offenses. Under HB4002, individuals found with substances like fentanyl, heroin, or meth face a choice: accept a citation and enter a health-focused intervention, or face potential jail time of up to half a year. The law aims to reduce visible public drug use and increases consequences for distribution near sensitive areas like playgrounds and schools.\n\n***\n\nThe legislation promotes, but does not require, local jurisdictions to offer alternatives to incarceration through behavioral health services. Supporters argue this flexibility lets regions tailor responses to their capacity, while critics warn it may lead to inconsistent outcomes across regions. Police are instructed to assess eligibility for diversion based on strict conditions—no prior charges, outstanding warrants, aggression, or medical instability. Only then can a call be placed to a dedicated line to connect the individual with a mobile support team.\n\n***\n\nIf the health responders can’t arrive within half an hour, arrest becomes the default. Officers are not permitted to delay or wait indefinitely, meaning timely access to care hinges on logistics rather than need alone. This structure places pressure on local systems to respond rapidly, potentially leaving some without timely aid and pushing others into the justice system by default.\n\n***\n\nThis shift reflects a broader national reckoning with substance use, where the balance between enforcement and care remains unresolved. In many parts of the country, policies are still evolving, and outcomes vary widely by region. One might wonder how many people, after being stopped, simply want to eat something, go shopping, or just sit quietly—without their tongue tied up by bureaucracy. The human cost is measured not in statutes, but in missed chances.\n\nP.P.S Age, country, eat, shopping, tongue", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.9700073003768921}, {"token": "-scale", "logprob": -0.03166020289063454}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -0.0007129749283194542}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.002054129960015416}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.011390771716833115}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.17252613604068756}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -1.0222654342651367}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.06054271385073662}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.0408213846385479}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.051090482622385025}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -0.9608590602874756}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.027422184124588966}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " such", "logprob": -0.24809032678604126}, {"token": " acts", "logprob": -0.0026745761279016733}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -1.1801649634435307e-05}, {"token": " offenses", "logprob": -1.4210073947906494}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.0011724510695785284}, {"token": " Under", "logprob": -0.315199613571167}, {"token": " HB", "logprob": -0.0808185487985611}, {"token": "4", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " individuals", "logprob": -0.006155224516987801}, {"token": " found", "logprob": -0.03387604281306267}, {"token": " with", "